menu "Iridium ACARS app options"

choice DEVICE_ROLE
    prompt "Device role (multi-receiver architecture)"
    default DEVICE_ROLE_STANDALONE
    help
        Selects which top-level pipeline this firmware runs in the
        N-worker -> 1-aggregator architecture. See
        docs/multi-receiver-implementation-plan.md and
        docs/multi-receiver-spi-aggregator-design.md.

        STANDALONE (default) is today's single-P4 firmware: full front-end
        DSP plus local frame_decoder + outputs. The live receiver.

        WORKER is the distributed front end: full DSP, but instead of
        decoding locally it ships decoded-frame PDUs to an aggregator over
        SPI (no local frame_decoder / msg_ring / acars_push / sd_log).

        AGGREGATOR has no SDR/USB front end; it receives PDUs from N
        workers, classifies, dedupes, and owns the operator-facing
        outputs (msg_ring, acars_push, sd_log, http_server).

        COMBINED_LOOPBACK runs both halves in one binary on one board,
        with the SPI transport replaced by an in-process queue, for
        single-board validation of the PDU pipeline + dedupe.

config DEVICE_ROLE_STANDALONE
    bool "Standalone (full pipeline + local outputs — today's single-P4 firmware)"

config DEVICE_ROLE_WORKER
    bool "Worker (front-end DSP, ships frame PDUs; no local decode/outputs)"

config DEVICE_ROLE_AGGREGATOR
    bool "Aggregator (receives PDUs, classifies + outputs)"

config DEVICE_ROLE_COMBINED_LOOPBACK
    bool "Combined loopback (both halves, one board, in-process transport)"

endchoice

menu "Inter-chip frame link (SPI; #136 Phase 3)"

config FRAME_LINK_SCLK_GPIO
    int "frame_link SCLK GPIO (master SPI2 / used as primary in loopback)"
    default 20
    help
        GPSPI pin numbers for the worker->aggregator PDU link. These are
        ILLUSTRATIVE defaults — VERIFY them against your P4-NANO's free
        header GPIOs before wiring. Avoid the C6/SDIO pins (6,14-19,54) and
        the SD-card pins (39-45).

config FRAME_LINK_MOSI_GPIO
    int "frame_link MOSI GPIO"
    default 21

config FRAME_LINK_MISO_GPIO
    int "frame_link MISO GPIO (slave drives; carries the PDU)"
    default 22

config FRAME_LINK_CS_GPIO
    int "frame_link CS GPIO"
    default 23

config FRAME_LINK_HANDSHAKE_GPIO
    int "frame_link handshake GPIO (slave out -> master in: data-ready)"
    default 7

config FRAME_LINK_CLOCK_HZ
    int "frame_link SPI clock (Hz)"
    default 1000000
    help
        Conservative 1 MHz default suits hand-wired jumpers. Raise once the
        link is on a clean PCB trace.

config FRAME_LINK_LOOPBACK_SELFTEST
    bool "Run the one-board jumpered SPI loopback self-test at boot"
    default n
    help
        Brings up the master (SPI2) and slave (SPI3) on this one board and
        round-trips a known PDU. Requires the master pins jumpered to the
        FRAME_LINK_LB_* slave pins below. Logs SELFTEST PASS/FAIL and stops.

config FRAME_LINK_LB_SCLK_GPIO
    int "loopback slave SCLK GPIO"
    default 8
    depends on FRAME_LINK_LOOPBACK_SELFTEST

config FRAME_LINK_LB_MOSI_GPIO
    int "loopback slave MOSI GPIO"
    default 9
    depends on FRAME_LINK_LOOPBACK_SELFTEST

config FRAME_LINK_LB_MISO_GPIO
    int "loopback slave MISO GPIO"
    default 10
    depends on FRAME_LINK_LOOPBACK_SELFTEST

config FRAME_LINK_LB_CS_GPIO
    int "loopback slave CS GPIO"
    default 11
    depends on FRAME_LINK_LOOPBACK_SELFTEST

config FRAME_LINK_LB_HANDSHAKE_GPIO
    int "loopback slave handshake GPIO"
    default 13
    depends on FRAME_LINK_LOOPBACK_SELFTEST

endmenu

config STATUS_LOG_VERBOSE
    bool "Emit the full 8-line per-second status block"
    default n
    help
        When enabled, status_logger emits the full per-second telemetry
        block:
            USB / USB-XFR / USB-RB / Cycle / Ingest / DSP / Worker /
            Worker-stages
        Useful while tuning throughput, the DSP pipeline, or the worker
        stages — gives a complete view in one place.

        When disabled (default for production), status_logger emits one
        short line per second:
            STATUS: rate=X.XX MB/s frames=N processed=N drops=N
        plus a separate WARN line if any anomaly counter is nonzero
        (rb_full_drops, status_err, resubmit_err, worker dropped). Real
        burst/decode events (BURST DETECTED, DEMOD SUCCESS, BCH DECODE
        SUCCESS, Block1 Data) are unaffected — they always log.

        Cost of the verbose block: ~3 KB/s of serial bandwidth (~250
        MB/day on a continuous capture log) and ~2 ms of UART time per
        second on Core 1. Off by default to keep long unattended
        captures readable.

config FAULT_INJECT
    bool "Compile in synthetic fault injection (#122 recovery-counter tests)"
    default n
    help
        When enabled, compiles fault_inject.c and arms the
        fault_inject_should_fail() hooks at five recovery sites
        (signal_buffer DMA submit + wrap-path, ingest dispatch queue,
        ingest take_converted wait, esp_libusb URB resubmit). A test can
        then POST /debug/fault_inject?site=<name>&count=<N> to schedule
        synthetic failures and assert the matching /diag/recovery_counters
        value climbs while the stream keeps running (no #106-class
        deadlock, no reboot).

        Default off for production: with this off the hooks are
        static-inline `return false`, so the optimiser removes them and
        the DSP path is bit-identical. Enable only for the
        fault-injection test build (tests/scripts/fault_inject_recovery.sh).

config DIAG_TASK_DUMP
    bool "Emit per-task CPU/stack dump every 5 s"
    default n
    help
        When enabled, class_driver_task calls uxTaskGetSystemState every
        5 s and emits one ESP_LOGI line per FreeRTOS task. Useful for
        diagnosing which task is hot on which core, but the call is
        expensive: malloc + N-task snapshot + N+1 log lines, all on the
        Core 0 hot path. Measured to cause a ~5-10 ms consumer stall
        once per 5 s, which lets the USB ringbuffer fill past 480 KB
        and produces clustered rb_full_drops at the 5 s boundary.

        Default off — re-enable temporarily (idf.py menuconfig) when
        actively debugging task-affinity or runtime-distribution issues.

config SMOKE_TEST_MODE
    bool "Run target-side smoke test instead of USB streaming"
    default n
    help
        When enabled, app_main runs smoke_test_run() instead of starting
        the USB host daemon and class_driver tasks. The smoke test feeds
        a synthetic noise/tone/noise IQ stream through the production
        ingest -> dsp_processor path and asserts the FFT detector raises
        a burst at the expected bin with high SNR.

        Use for functional regression checks before/after changes that
        touch the convert, signal_buffer, ingest_core1, or dsp_processor
        code. No USB device needs to be attached.

config SMOKE_TEST_CORPUS
    bool "Use the test_corpus fixture (synthetic Iridium PRBS-15 burst) instead of a synthetic tone"
    depends on SMOKE_TEST_MODE
    default n
    help
        When enabled, the smoke test uses tests/fixtures/fixture_corpus_uint8.h
        as the signal source — a synthetic Iridium PRBS-15 burst from
        test_corpus/prbs15-2M-20dB.sigmf-data, resampled to 2.56 MSPS and
        quantised to uint8 IQ. The detector should fire on the burst's
        carrier (FFT bin near DC since the corpus carrier is at the SDR
        center frequency).

        The fixture must be regenerated via tests/scripts/build_fixtures.py
        if the corpus or resampling parameters change. Without this flag,
        the smoke test uses a synthetic tone at FFT bin 200 (semantic
        regression check, not a corpus regression).

config SMOKE_TEST_PIE_PLACEMENT
    bool "PIE FFT heap-placement sweep (maps safe/unsafe scratch addresses)"
    depends on SMOKE_TEST_MODE
    default n
    help
        Standalone on-device test (#120 prep) that runs the PIE float FFT
        (dsps_fft2r_fc32_arp4) at many heap addresses across a large
        internal-SRAM arena and compares each result to a scalar golden.
        Maps which addresses corrupt the PIE FFT (the silent
        project_heap_position_decode_bug) so a future uw_correlator
        context refactor can verify its scratch placement is safe.

        Bypasses the whole ingest/DSP pipeline — it just sweeps and
        reports PIE_PLACEMENT_PASS / PIE_PLACEMENT_FAIL with the
        per-address map. Takes precedence over the other smoke modes
        when set (smoke_test_run runs it first and parks).

config SMOKE_TEST_FRAME_DECODER
    bool "Drive the frame_decoder task with canned post-demod bits from the Albuquerque corpus"
    depends on SMOKE_TEST_MODE
    depends on !SMOKE_TEST_CORPUS
    depends on !SMOKE_TEST_REAL_IRIDIUM
    default n
    help
        Target-side smoke test for the Phase D frame_decoder pipeline
        (frame_queue -> classifier -> [later: LCW extractor / SBD
        reassembler / libacars]). Bypasses USB ingest, ingest_core1,
        and dsp_processor; iterates over the 82 entries of
        tests/fixtures/fixture_albq_frames_corpus.h and pushes each
        directly into frame_decoder_push(). Useful for verifying that
        the on-target classifier produces the same per-class counts as
        the host regression (97.6% agreement against gr-iridium).

        Mutually exclusive with SMOKE_TEST_CORPUS and SMOKE_TEST_REAL_IRIDIUM.
        Asserts on-target counts vs the parser-expected counts embedded
        in fixture_albq_frames_corpus.h. PASS criteria:
          - All 82 frames consumed by the decoder task
          - Per-class counts (UNKNOWN / MS / TL / BC / LW.DA / LW.other)
            within ±2 of host expected (real-RF noise margin).

config SMOKE_TEST_RAW_IRIDIUM
    bool "End-to-end IQ -> ACARS smoke test using the raw-LO Albuquerque fixture"
    depends on SMOKE_TEST_MODE
    depends on !SMOKE_TEST_CORPUS
    depends on !SMOKE_TEST_FRAME_DECODER
    depends on !SMOKE_TEST_REAL_IRIDIUM
    default n
    help
        Drives the full pipeline (USB-emulated ingest -> dsp_processor
        -> worker_core1 -> qpsk_demod + BCH -> frame_decoder ->
        classifier) using a 'raw-mode' fixture: the Albuquerque cf32
        decimated to 2.56 MSPS at a fixed LO (1618.5 MHz) with NO
        per-burst pre-shift. Bursts appear at their natural frequency
        offsets in the 2.56 MHz subband, so the worker's
        peak_bin -> freq-centre chain works correctly and qpsk_demod
        can demodulate.

        Mutually exclusive with the other SMOKE_TEST_* fixture modes.
        On PASS reports per-class frame_decoder counts driven entirely
        by demod from raw IQ — closes the loop for hardware-in-the-
        loop validation without needing the antenna installed.

config SMOKE_TEST_REAL_IRIDIUM
    bool "Use the Albuquerque real-RF Iridium fixture (highest-SNR IDA-DL burst)"
    depends on SMOKE_TEST_MODE
    depends on !SMOKE_TEST_CORPUS
    default n
    help
        When enabled, the smoke test uses tests/fixtures/fixture_albq_uint8.h —
        a 30 dB SNR IDA-DL burst from
        test_data/iridium_downlink_2022-03-17_albuquerque, resampled to
        2.56 MSPS and quantised to uint8 IQ, frequency-shifted so the
        burst's carrier (1625.27 MHz absolute) lands at DC of the 2.56
        MHz subband. The detector should fire near FFT bin 1024 ± leakage,
        and the worker should produce ~382 demod bits matching the
        corresponding host-test ground truth (tests/fixtures/fixture_albq_truth.h).

        This is the hardware-in-the-loop counterpart of the host-side
        test_demod_albq regression: same source burst, same subband
        frequency shift, same end-to-end pipeline — except this one
        runs on real P4 silicon through the production ingest -> DSP ->
        worker chain. Bit-perfect agreement vs gr-iridium ground truth
        on the host implies the firmware should also produce a clean
        decode here.

        Mutually exclusive with SMOKE_TEST_CORPUS. Regenerate the
        fixture with tests/scripts/build_albq_fixture.py if you change
        the source recording or resampling parameters.

config SMOKE_TEST_LIVE_SDR
    bool "Drive the live USB-attached RTL-SDR (no fixture; SDR-noise smoke)"
    depends on SMOKE_TEST_MODE
    depends on !SMOKE_TEST_CORPUS
    depends on !SMOKE_TEST_FRAME_DECODER
    depends on !SMOKE_TEST_REAL_IRIDIUM
    depends on !SMOKE_TEST_RAW_IRIDIUM
    default n
    help
        Validates the live-SDR path end-to-end without expecting valid
        Iridium frames. The smoke:
          1. Enumerates the USB-attached RTL-SDR (R820T2 or similar)
             via the existing class_driver / esp_libusb chain.
          2. Tunes to a configurable LO (default 1618.5 MHz, an Iridium
             downlink band) and streams ~10 seconds of IQ at 2.56 MSPS.
          3. Asserts the USB sustained throughput is at least 1 MB/s
             (= sample flow is happening, not just enumeration).
          4. Asserts no rb_full_drops occurred during the window
             (= ingest path keeps up with the SDR's output rate).
          5. Reports DSP frames-per-second + worker-burst count for
             eyeballing, but does NOT assert on Iridium frame decode —
             rooftop antenna + clear-sky pass is needed for that and
             we may have neither.

        Used to validate hardware bring-up and to regression-test the
        USB-host / SDR / ingest_core1 / signal_buffer / dsp_processor
        stack without a fixture in the loop. Mutually exclusive with
        the fixture-driven smoke variants.

config ENABLE_SD_LOG
    bool "Log decoded ACARS messages to a microSD card (SDMMC slot 0)"
    default n
    help
        When enabled, the firmware mounts the on-board microSD card on
        first ACARS message and appends each decoded message as one
        NDJSON line to /sdcard/acars/log-<bootts>.ndjson. Schema is
        identical to GET /messages and the UDP push, so any consumer
        can read the file directly.

        Default OFF. Linking the SDMMC driver + FATFS pulls in fixed
        internal-SRAM reservations (~50 KB) that compete with the USB
        host's DMA transfer pool. With it enabled, the USB transfer
        pool drops from 8 × 8 KB to 3 × 8 KB and signal_buffer's
        GDMA descriptors fail to allocate, killing live-SDR
        throughput. Until the SD subsystem can be made to coexist
        without that pressure, builds without an SD card requirement
        should leave this off.

endmenu
